# Cross-Platform Support for Claude Plan Viewer

## Current State

The codebase is **already mostly cross-platform**:

| Component | Status | Implementation |
|-----------|--------|----------------|
| Plans directory | ✅ Works | `join(homedir(), ".claude", "plans")` - uses Node.js cross-platform APIs |
| Path joining | ✅ Works | Uses `node:path` `join()` which handles separators correctly |
| Open in editor | ✅ Works | Already has `darwin`, `win32`, and Linux (`xdg-open`) support |

## Issue Found

**The `extractProject()` function uses unreliable regex heuristics** instead of Claude Code's actual metadata.

## Discovery: Claude Code Metadata Structure

Claude Code stores project metadata in `~/.claude/projects/`:

```
~/.claude/projects/
├── -Users-helge-code-plans-viewer/       # Directory name encodes project path
│   ├── 9b29113d-c7a6-49ec-be57-02315cd04cb2.jsonl
│   └── agent-*.jsonl
├── -Users-helge-code-crescat-website/
│   └── *.jsonl
```

**Key insight**: JSONL files contain a `slug` field that matches plan filenames:
- Plan file: `quizzical-wondering-hoare.md`
- JSONL entry: `"slug":"quizzical-wondering-hoare"`
- Project directory: `-Users-helge-code-crescat-website` → project = `crescat-website`

## Recommended Approach

**Use Claude Code's own metadata** to map plans to projects (100% reliable, cross-platform).

### Algorithm

1. Read all plan files from `~/.claude/plans/*.md`
2. Build a slug→project lookup table by scanning `~/.claude/projects/*/`:
   - For each project directory, scan JSONL files for `"slug":"..."` entries
   - Extract project name from directory: `-Users-helge-code-PROJECT` → `PROJECT`
3. For each plan, use its filename (slug) to look up the project

### New Implementation in `index.ts`

```typescript
const PROJECTS_DIR = join(homedir(), ".claude", "projects");

interface ProjectMapping {
  [slug: string]: string;  // slug → project name
}

async function buildProjectMapping(): Promise<ProjectMapping> {
  const mapping: ProjectMapping = {};

  try {
    const projectDirs = await readdir(PROJECTS_DIR);

    for (const dir of projectDirs) {
      // Extract project name from directory: -Users-helge-code-PROJECT → PROJECT
      const projectName = extractProjectFromDir(dir);
      if (!projectName) continue;

      const dirPath = join(PROJECTS_DIR, dir);
      const stats = await stat(dirPath);
      if (!stats.isDirectory()) continue;

      // Scan JSONL files for slug entries
      const files = await readdir(dirPath);
      for (const file of files.filter(f => f.endsWith('.jsonl'))) {
        const content = await Bun.file(join(dirPath, file)).text();
        // Find all slug entries in this JSONL
        const slugMatches = content.matchAll(/"slug":"([\w-]+)"/g);
        for (const match of slugMatches) {
          mapping[match[1]] = projectName;
        }
      }
    }
  } catch {
    // Projects dir may not exist, return empty mapping
  }

  return mapping;
}

function extractProjectFromDir(dirname: string): string | null {
  // Directory format: -Users-helge-code-PROJECT or -home-user-projects-PROJECT
  // On Windows: -C-Users-name-code-PROJECT
  const parts = dirname.split('-').filter(Boolean);

  // Skip common path components to find the project name
  const skipWords = ['users', 'home', 'c', 'code', 'projects', 'dev', 'work', 'repos'];

  // Find last meaningful segment (the project name)
  for (let i = parts.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    if (!skipWords.includes(parts[i].toLowerCase())) {
      return parts[i];
    }
  }
  return parts[parts.length - 1] || null;
}
```

### Update `loadPlans()` function

```typescript
