# CSV Column Width Fix: Prevent Truncation During Editing

## Problem Statement

**Current Bug:** When editing a cell, the column width is set to match only the edited cell's length. This causes truncation of other cells in the same column if they contain longer content.

**Example:**
- Column has cells: "hello world" (11 chars), "test" (4 chars), "example" (7 chars)
- Initial column width: 11 chars (max of all cells)
- Edit "test" cell → column shrinks to 4 chars
- "hello world" and "example" are now truncated

## Research: How Tabular Editors Handle Column Width

### CSS Table Behavior
- **`table-layout: auto`** (default): Column width determined by scanning ALL cells, finds maximum content width
- **`table-layout: fixed`**: Column width set explicitly, content may overflow/truncate
- Source: [MDN table-layout](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/table-layout), [CSS-Tricks](https://css-tricks.com/almanac/properties/t/table-layout/)

### Spreadsheet Behavior (Excel/Google Sheets)
- **Auto-fit**: Scans ALL cells in column to find widest content
- **Not automatic during editing**: Users must manually trigger "Fit to data"
- **Column width remains stable**: Doesn't shrink automatically while typing
- Source: [Google Sheets Autofit Guide](https://coefficient.io/google-sheets-tutorials/how-to-autofit-column-width-in-google-sheets), [Spreadsheet Point](https://spreadsheetpoint.com/autofit-column-width-google-sheets/)

### Design Principle
**Columns can GROW but should never SHRINK below the width needed for existing content**

## Current Architecture

**Initial Column Width Calculation** (src/csv/model.rs:383-399):
- `calculate_column_widths()` scans first 100 rows
- Finds maximum cell width per column (4-40 chars)
- Called once when CSV state is created

**Current (Buggy) Edit-Time Behavior** (src/update/csv.rs:380-391):
```rust
fn update_column_width_for_edit(csv: &mut CsvState, content_len: usize) {
    const MIN_WIDTH: usize = 4;
    const EDIT_MAX_WIDTH: usize = 32;

    // BUG: Sets width to edited cell length only!
    *width = content_len.clamp(MIN_WIDTH, EDIT_MAX_WIDTH);
}
```

**Problem:** Ignores widths of other cells in the column.

## Solution Design

### Option A: Grow-Only During Edit (Recommended)

**Behavior:**
- During editing: Column width = `max(edited_cell_length, current_column_width)`
- Column can GROW to fit new content (up to EDIT_MAX_WIDTH)
- Column NEVER shrinks below existing width (prevents truncation)
- On confirm: Optionally recalculate full column width
- On cancel: Keep current width (or optionally recalculate)

**Advantages:**
- Simple, minimal code change
- Prevents truncation bug
- Predictable behavior
- Fast (no column scanning during typing)

**Disadvantages:**
- Columns don't automatically shrink after deleting long content
- Users would need manual "auto-fit" command to shrink columns

### Option B: Recalculate Column on Every Edit

**Behavior:**
- On every keystroke: Scan ALL cells in edited column
- Set column width to maximum found (4-32 chars)

**Advantages:**
- Always accurate column width
- Columns shrink when appropriate

**Disadvantages:**
- Performance cost: O(n) scan per keystroke for large CSVs
- May cause visual jitter as columns resize
- Counterintuitive UX (columns jumping around while typing)

### Option C: Recalculate Only on Confirm/Cancel

**Behavior:**
- During editing: Use grow-only approach (Option A)
- On confirm/cancel: Scan column and set accurate width

**Advantages:**
- Best UX: stable during typing, accurate after edit
- Performance: only scan on confirm/cancel, not per keystroke

**Disadvantages:**
- More complex implementation
- Still requires full column scan

## Recommended Approach: **Option A + Manual Recalculation**

Implement Option A for editing + add a helper function for future auto-fit command:

1. **During edit**: Grow-only (prevents truncation, fast)
2. **On confirm**: Keep grown width (simple, no surprises)
3. **On cancel**: Restore original width (user expectation)
4. **Future enhancement**: Add "auto-fit column" command that recalculates

